WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:04.560
Once upon a time, Uma Thurman was Hollywood's
hottest starlet.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:10.150
After "Pulp Fiction" wowed critics and audiences
alike in 1994, Thurman seemed destined for

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:15.410
box office greatness, but that fiercely shining
star has dimmed in recent years.

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:16.410
Here's why.

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:21.190
Chances are, the first films that comes to
mind when you think of Uma Thurman are "Pulp

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:25.480
Fiction" and "Kill Bill," both written and
directed by Quentin Tarantino.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.640
Although she had over a dozen credits before
"Pulp Fiction," Tarantino's Oscar-winning

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.620
crime classic was arguably the film that shot
Thurman to stardom.

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:35.230
"Now, I wanna dance.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:36.570
I wanna win.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:37.670
I want that trophy.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.440
So dance good."

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.690
After its success, she split her time between
shooting for Oscar nods with films like "Les

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:48.730
Miserables" in 1998 and working on somewhat
more crowd-pleasing but forgettable flicks

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:53.490
like 1996's "The Truth About Cats and Dogs"
and 2000's "The Golden Bowl."

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:57.890
In the early 2000s, as her star seemed to
be dimming a little, Thurman teamed up with

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:03.760
Tarantino again for the action-packed, revenge-driven
"Kill Bill '' films, released in 2003 and

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:07.530
2004 to critical acclaim and box office success.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:11.610
As with "Pulp Fiction," the movies triggered
a surge in Thurman's star status, which again

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:14.950
seemed to fade a little after the film's success
died down.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.390
Is Tarantino the key to Thurman's biggest
box office success?

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:23.110
He's definitely been a big boost to it, and
maybe one day the duo will finally get back

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:26.360
together for the much-discussed "Kill Bill
Volume 3."

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.510
Thurman fends off hordes of sword-swinging
gangsters in "Kill Bill," but in real life,

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:35.460
she's had to deal with something much worse:
A child custody battle.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:40.950
According to TMZ, Thurman's ex Arpad Busson,
the father of her youngest child, Luna, dropped

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:45.549
an ultimatum on her in 2016, threatening that
if she films a movie, she won't be able to

00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:47.060
see Luna anymore.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.580
If there's ever been a reason to turn down
a movie role, that's gotta be it, even if

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.980
the circumstances aren't your fault.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:57.870
Earlier in 2016, TMZ also reported that Busson
had filed a request with the court to get

00:01:57.870 --> 00:01:59.810
more custody of their daughter.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:03.530
With all that time in front of a judge, it's
no wonder Thurman doesn't have many chances

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:04.979
to get in front of a camera.

00:02:05.979 --> 00:02:09.959
For years, Uma Thurman has been an outspoken
member of a range of charities.

00:02:09.959 --> 00:02:14.129
Not only is she a board member for Room to
Grow, a charity tasked with helping children

00:02:14.129 --> 00:02:20.540
born into poverty, but she's also done advertising
work as a spokesperson for USAID, an organization

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:25.650
focused on helping those affected by war,
famine, and drought in the Horn of Africa.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:31.060
In early 2016, the actress also put in face
time at an AIDS charity event in Hong Kong.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:34.409
With all this humanitarian work, who has time
to crank out blockbusters?

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:40.099
Though she's certainly had her hits, Uma Thurman's
one of those actors who's historically had

00:02:40.099 --> 00:02:41.940
a little trouble selling tickets.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:46.230
Her filmography boasts a handful of films
that grossed roughly $100 million or more

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:50.680
during their theatrical runs, but much of
her Hollywood history is one of disappointing

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:54.249
financial returns, at least when it comes
to leading roles.

00:02:54.249 --> 00:02:58.159
While it's true that a number of her movies
have been lower-budget efforts that weren't

00:02:58.159 --> 00:03:02.980
aiming for blockbuster status, she's also
been in a number of high-profile duds, including

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:06.249
"Gattaca," "My Super Ex-Girlfriend," and "The
Avengers."

00:03:06.249 --> 00:03:10.430
That doesn't mean she doesn't have more hits
in her, but it definitely could make certain

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:12.859
executives think twice about casting decisions.

00:03:14.069 --> 00:03:18.889
Beyond all the celebrity gossip and box office
numbers, Thurman is also still a mom to three

00:03:18.889 --> 00:03:23.169
children, and anyone who's ever been in that
situation knows that, millionaire or not,

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:25.919
it takes a lot of work to raise kids on your
own.

00:03:25.919 --> 00:03:28.169
By her own account, it's been hectic.

00:03:28.169 --> 00:03:30.340
As she told London's The Times in 2009:

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:33.239
"My big wish now is to make a little time
for myself.

00:03:33.239 --> 00:03:35.609
I think many women, working women, get this.

00:03:35.609 --> 00:03:39.389
I mean, how do you justify that hour and a
half to yourself?"

00:03:39.389 --> 00:03:44.109
Between raising her kids, working, doing charity
appearances, and any number of other obligations,

00:03:44.109 --> 00:03:48.559
it makes sense that packing her schedule full
of movie roles might not be high on Thurman's

00:03:48.559 --> 00:03:50.180
list of priorities these days.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:56.060
Throughout the 1990s and 2000s, Uma Thurman
cranked out an average of two feature-length

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:57.810
film projects per year.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:02.949
With the dawn of the 2010s, though, she began
to diversify a little, and take on more small-screen

00:04:02.949 --> 00:04:03.949
work.

00:04:03.949 --> 00:04:08.890
In recent years she's starred or guest-starred
in several TV series, including NBC's 2015

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:14.030
version of "The Slap," six episodes of Bravo's
"Imposters," and the short-lived Netflix organ

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:17.200
transplant-based horror series "Chambers."

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.560
Thurman is currently in the process of shooting
the thriller series "Suspicion," which will

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.330
air on Apple TV+.

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:27.150
Thanks to the streaming era, more and more
big-screen stars in front of and behind the

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:31.780
camera are using television as a space to
explore new stories, and they often win a

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:33.590
lot of acclaim for that work.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:38.710
Though she hasn't made the leap to TV completely,
it's easy to see Thurman aiming for a small-screen

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:42.020
reinvention, and finding fresh, exciting new
work in the process.

00:04:43.099 --> 00:04:47.350
Though she never entirely left the big screen,
and she's still landing roles on the small

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:51.490
screen as well, mass media's not the only
place for Uma Thurman to show off her acting

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:52.490
talents.

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:55.259
In recent years, she's also made a return
to the stage.

00:04:55.259 --> 00:05:00.789
In 2017, she played the lead in "The Parisian
Woman," a Broadway production written by "House

00:05:00.789 --> 00:05:02.449
of Cards" creator Beau Willimon.

00:05:02.449 --> 00:05:07.349
Two years later, she performed the lead role
in "Ghosts," penned by 19th century playwright

00:05:07.349 --> 00:05:10.310
Henrik Ibsen, at the Williamstown Theatre
Festival.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:12.199
As she told the Boston Globe at the time:

00:05:12.199 --> 00:05:15.950
"Ibsen's work, having studied it in high school
a bit, was one of the things that drew me

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:17.220
to want to be an actress.

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:21.979
Ibsen's women are so fascinating and deep
and complicated and truly require every scrap

00:05:21.979 --> 00:05:23.830
of talent one has to get through them."

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:29.449
Any actor who survives in Hollywood for nearly
40 years is going to have ups and downs in

00:05:29.449 --> 00:05:33.240
their career, and Uma Thurman is no different
in that regard.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:37.509
Even if audiences haven't gobbled them all,
she's still making films, and her recent output

00:05:37.509 --> 00:05:41.890
includes everything from young adult horror
story "Down a Dark Hall" to the shocker "The

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:45.509
House that Jack Built" to the R-rated comedy
"The War with Grandpa."

00:05:45.509 --> 00:05:50.500
None of those films were mega-hits, but Thurman's
plainly maintained her staying power, and

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:52.300
it goes beyond just acting.

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:56.440
This is an actor who does charity work, raises
her children, speaks out about mistreatment

00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:01.159
by everyone from Quentin Tarantino to Harvey
Weinstein, and still finds time to deliver

00:06:01.159 --> 00:06:03.550
solid acting work along the way.

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:07.389
Even if you don't see Uma Thurman around as
much as you used to, one thing's for sure:

00:06:07.389 --> 00:06:09.280
She never really went away.

